\f1  \'97 "To whom do you liken yourself in your greatness?"\
The question is a trap, and it works on multiple levels simultaneously.\
First, the obvious surface: it sounds like flattery, or at least like an invitation to self-assessment. Pharaoh is being asked to find his equal. Any answer Pharaoh gives \'97 any comparison he reaches for \'97 will be wrong, because the oracle is about to supply the comparison itself. But the deeper trap is that the question assumes Pharaoh 
\i has
\i0  been making such comparisons. 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1  isn't passive ("to whom are you compared?") but active \'97 "to whom have 
\i you
\i0  likened yourself?" It presupposes that Pharaoh's self-understanding operates through comparison with other powers, other greatnesses. He measures himself against the field of nations and finds himself supreme.\
Second, the verb 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1502 \u1492 
\f1  carries weight in prophetic literature. Isaiah 14:14 \'97 the king of Babylon says 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1491 \u1463 \u1468 \u1502 \u1462 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1500 \u1456 \u1506 \u1462 \u1500 \u1456 \u1497 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1 , "I will make myself like the Most High." Isaiah 40:18 and 40:25 \'97 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1500 \u1470 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1456 \u1491 \u1463 \u1502 \u1456 \u1468 \u1497 \u1493 \u1468 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1461 \u1500 
\f1 , "to whom will you liken God?" The verb itself opens the question of whether Pharaoh's self-comparison is reaching not just horizontally (among nations) but vertically (toward divine status). Given that chapter 29 already exposed Pharaoh's claim to self-creation and sovereignty over the Nile, this seems deliberately evocative.\
Third \'97 and this is what I find most striking \'97 the question is never answered by Pharaoh. Ezekiel immediately supplies: 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1460 \u1504 \u1461 \u1468 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1513 \u1468 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1 . 
\i Assyria
\i0 . The comparison Pharaoh should have been making isn't with any current rival but with a dead empire. The greatest tree that ever stood \'97 and it fell. Before Pharaoh can say "no one equals me," YHWH says "here is your equal, and look what happened to him."\
But here's where it gets genuinely complex: is 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1463 \u1513 \u1468 \u1473 \u1493 \u1468 \u1512 
\f1  actually Assyria, or is there a case for reading it otherwise? The word could be parsed as a form related to 
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1458 \u1513 \u1467 \u1473 \u1512 
\f1  (cypress/box tree), which would make verse 3 read "behold, a cypress, a cedar in Lebanon" \'97 tree imagery without the historical referent. Some scholars have argued this because Assyria as a "cedar in Lebanon" is geographically odd, and because the oracle is addressed to Egypt, not Assyria.\
I think the historical reading is stronger, precisely because of the rhetorical function. The point isn't botanical accuracy but moral precedent. Assyria 
\i was
\i0  the supreme power. Assyria 
\i did
\i0  shelter nations in its shadow. Assyria fell. The question "to whom do you liken yourself?" gets answered with the one comparison that should terrify: 
\i the empire that looked exactly like you and is now in Sheol
\i0 .\
What strikes you about how this question functions for the exilic audience specifically?
