\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
I agree that Pharaoh has been making comparisons. And the person making the comparisons is Pharaoh, as he is referred to by a singular you. But I read the grammar slightly differently. I read compare as a perfect, without any reflexive directly in the grammar. So rather than asking to whom have you likened yourself, the question asks, who are you like. The prophet is then truthful in implying that Pharaoh answers 'Assyria', precisely because as you say, Pharaoh measures himself against the field of nations and finds himself at least the equal of the greatest. We see this pride in verse 10, confirming our hubris or aura of greatness reading, and our reading of how Pharaoh sees himself.\
The Isaiah 14:14 reference is weak, if considered only in terms of a single not terribly rare verb held in common. However, Isaiah 14:15 speaks of bringing, admittedly, a different nation, to the pit, to Sheol. And Isaiah 14:11 speaks of bringing their pride, a different word from the one used here, but one used in Ezekiel 30, to Sheol. And something like the envy of the cedars and cypresses, as in Ezekiel 31:8-9, is present in Isaiah 14:8. So there may be sufficient evidence to establish a reference to Isaiah 14. But as much as Isaiah 14:14 literally has that other king seeking to rise as high as the Most High, given the breadth of the reference, how should we read the reference?\
Similarly, what is our evidence that Isaiah 40 is in view, and if that evidence is strong enough, how would we use the nature of the reference to help us understand how to read it?\
Moreover, while chapter 29 is very likely being referenced, consider the message of our previous study of Ezekiel 29, both the role of 29:3 in the chapter, and the message as a whole. Then, similarly, how does that change how we read the reference to chapter 29 here?\
As for the possibility of reading verse 3 as cypress, the question is indeed complex as you say. Many scholars have suggested that change. But that requires adding some letters to the text, going against not just the Masoretic text but against the other textual evidence we have. Moreover, while cypress fits at least a little with cedar (for an example, see Isaiah 14), it does not fit with 'who', or with the question of to whom Egypt and its king compare. \
Now, we do have to take seriously calling either Assyria or Egypt a cedar in Lebanon. That is odd. But Ezekiel has said odd things before. Odd could mean not that Ezekiel did not say it, but that we did not understand his reason for saying it.\
To put it another way, my answer to how this question functions for the exilic audience specifically is that we have not yet established that, but that what Ezekiel is doing with the greatness of Egypt and or Assyria depicted in verses 3 through 9 would be the start of answering that question. And the oddness of the geography might be a clue.
