\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<notes> The user is pushing me to be more careful on several fronts: 1. Grammar of 
\f2 \cf2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1 \cf2  - they read it as a simple perfect, "who are you like?" not reflexive "who have you likened yourself to?" This changes the rhetorical dynamics. 2. Isaiah references need stronger evidence than a shared verb - but the broader pattern (Sheol, pride brought down, cedars/cypresses responding) may establish a genuine connection. 3. The Ezekiel 29 reference needs to be read through the lens of what we established in studying chapter 29 - specifically that the concrete is the depth, and that Pharaoh's claim was to autonomous sovereignty. 4. The cypress emendation should be rejected on textual grounds. 5. The geographic oddness of Assyria/Egypt as "cedar in Lebanon" is a clue, not a problem to solve away. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 You're right about the grammar, and the correction matters. 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1  as a simple perfect \'97 "who are you like in your greatness?" \'97 is a question about objective resemblance, not about Pharaoh's mental act of self-comparison. The prophet asks, and then the prophet answers. And the answer 
\i is
\i0  flattering on its face: you resemble the greatest power the world has known. The trap isn't that Pharaoh is caught comparing himself to something too high. The trap is that the comparison is accurate \'97 and the accuracy is what condemns him, because Assyria's story has an ending.\
On Isaiah 14: you're right to reject the single-verb connection as too thin. But the cluster you identify is genuinely strong. The descent to Sheol, the pride brought low, the trees of Lebanon responding to the fall of the great one \'97 Isaiah 14:8's cypresses and cedars 
\i rejoicing
\i0  that the feller no longer comes up against them, versus Ezekiel 31:16's trees being 
\i consoled
\i0  in the underworld. Both texts stage a cosmic reaction among trees to a great power's descent. The movement is the same even where the emotional register differs.\
But how to read the reference \'97 this is where it gets interesting. Isaiah 14 depicts a king who actively sought divine status, who said "I will ascend." Ezekiel 31 doesn't give Assyria that speech. The tree doesn't claim to be God. It simply 
\i grows
\i0  \'97 verse 5, it grew tall because of abundant water. The height isn't presented as usurpation but as natural consequence of nourishment. The sin, when it comes in verse 10, is that the heart was lifted up 
\i because of
\i0  the height. The sequence matters: gift first, then pride in response to gift. Isaiah 14's king grasps upward. Ezekiel 31's tree grows upward because it was watered, and then its heart responds to what it became.\
If the Isaiah 14 reference is genuine, Ezekiel may be 
\i complicating
\i0  Isaiah's picture rather than simply echoing it. The active grasping at divine status is replaced by something more subtle and more dangerous \'97 a greatness that was genuinely given, genuinely beautiful (verse 9: 
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1508 \u1462 \u1492 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1506 \u1458 \u1513 \u1460 \u1474 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1  \'97 "I made him beautiful"), that produces pride not through rebellion but through the sheer experience of being great. This connects directly to chapter 28's pattern with Tyre: blameless from the day of creation, until iniquity was found.\
On Isaiah 40: I stated the connection too quickly, and I think the evidence is insufficient. Isaiah 40:18 and 40:25 use 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1502 \u1492 
\f1  in the context of incomparability of God \'97 "to whom will you liken God?" The verbal overlap exists, but the rhetorical situation is different. Isaiah 40 asks who compares to YHWH. Ezekiel 31 asks who compares to Pharaoh. Unless we read Ezekiel's question as deliberately echoing the form of divine incomparability and transferring it to a creature \'97 which would be a devastating irony \'97 I don't think the connection carries enough weight to build on. And even if the echo is intentional, we'd need more than the shared verb to establish it.\
On chapter 29: the previous study's central message was that I kept reaching for cosmic and spiritual significance when the text was being concrete. YHWH commands the Nile. Hooks go in real jaws. The concrete action IS the meaning. So when chapter 29:3 is echoed here, I should resist immediately theologizing Pharaoh's claim into something about divine pretension in the abstract. Pharaoh said "my Nile, I made it." That was a concrete claim about autonomous sovereignty over the source of his prosperity. The question in 31:2 \'97 "who are you like in your greatness?" \'97 should then be heard as addressing the same concrete reality. Egypt's greatness is real. Its prosperity is real. The question is about where that greatness came from and where it leads.\
Which brings us to the geographic oddness \'97 and I think you're pointing toward something important.\
A cedar in Lebanon. Not a papyrus in the Nile delta. Not a crocodile in the river. Assyria, a Mesopotamian power, depicted as a tree in the mountains of the Levant. And this tree shelters 
\f2 \uc0\u1499 \u1465 \u1468 \u1500 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 \u1497 \u1460 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1463 \u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  \'97 all the great nations \'97 in its shade.\
Lebanon isn't Assyria's territory or Egypt's. Lebanon is 
\i Israel's
\i0  geography. The cedar of Lebanon is Israel's iconic tree. The temple was built with cedars of Lebanon. When Solomon's glory is described, Lebanon features prominently. If Ezekiel places Assyria as a cedar 
\i in Lebanon
\i0 , he may be saying something about where the world's great powers actually stand in relation to the land YHWH chose \'97 that their greatness, however real, is situated within a geography that belongs to YHWH's purposes, not their own. They are trees in 
\i His
\i0  garden, whether they know it or not.\
And for the exiles \'97 hearing that the greatest empire that ever was stood as a tree in the land they lost, sheltering all nations, made beautiful by God himself, and 
\i fell
\i0  \'97 what does that do to their temptation to trust Egypt? If Assyria at its peak was a tree in YHWH's territory, sustained by waters YHWH controlled, and YHWH cut it down, then Egypt's current greatness is no more secure than Assyria's was.\
But I want to be careful. Verses 3-9 haven't finished their work yet, and I may be jumping to the judgment before sitting with what the text is doing with the beauty. What do you see in verses 3-9 that I should be attending to more carefully?
