Right, the switch of grammatical addressee is subtle. The because hints that there is a problem. But the tree, as a tree, growing, has been presented only positively. Still, with the because, addressing Pharaoh as growing in height suggests this is the problem. With the third person switch, we are back in the tree analogy. Now applied to Egypt? Has it always also spoken of Egypt? I do not think we have settled exactly in what way the tree now refers to Egypt, but it is clear it now does in some way. \
But it is the heart of Pharaoh lifted up that is the key to the justice of this accusation, and the rhetoric as it would have functioned even before the punishment fell historically. Heart lifted up is an accusation of pride. Deuteronomy 8 warned God's people to be careful not to let their hearts be lifted up in this way, thinking that their power got them their wealth, not the provision of their God. And Deuteronomy 17 warned that when God's people had a human king, their king was not to rely on Egypt, and he was to read God's law and fear the LORD his God rather than lifting up his heart.\
And yet, both the Bible and all the history since are quite clear - it is all too easy for a heart to be lifted up. Whether the smallest person or the greatest nation, it is all too natural to attribute one's success to what one chose or did, not to what one was given by God.\
And so the punishment, the sentence given for this pride, instructs, both in what it consists of and how it is described.
