It is not just the individuals constituting nations who are mortal. The trees go down to Sheol. And trees, in this metaphor, are nations. History is a graveyard of tombstones of dead nations. Many have exalted themselves, telling themselves that they were better than other nations, as in the oracles to various nations in Ezekiel 25. Many have engaged in bloody economic practices like the Tyre of Ezekiel 26 to 28. Many have engaged in bloody wars, power projection of the sort discussed somewhat directly in Ezekiel 29, or indirectly by the shade metaphor of this chapter; for in reality, the vassals of Assyria had been brought under its dominion by war and the fear of mass impalement and deportation. Since that time, many nations have brought themselves to bloody revolution, thinking that bringing down the current system will bring eternal greatness like the heavens. But objectively - what was the result? A graveyard. The people dead. The national greatness brought low.\
