This is concrete. The text is describing observable historical patterns. As with Ezekiel 29's lesson \'97 the concrete IS the depth. </notes>\
You've cut through something I was still abstracting. I described verse 13's birds on the fallen trunk as a poetic image. You're describing what actually happens when empires die. The Persians consumed what Babylon left. The successor kingdoms carved up Alexander's empire. The barbarian peoples settled on Rome's corpse and built from its ruins. The birds nesting on the fallen trunk isn't metaphor reaching for something spiritual. It's what the next century looks like after a great power collapses.\
And the shade \'97 I should have been more concrete about this. I described it as "actual provision" in verse 6, as if Assyria sheltered nations benevolently. But Assyria's shade was the shadow of an empire that flayed captives alive and displayed their skins on city walls. Nations dwelt under that shadow because the alternative was annihilation. The shelter was real in the sense that vassals survived and even prospered within the system. But the mechanism of that shelter was terror. When verse 12 says the peoples "descended from his shade and abandoned him," that's what happens when the threat of impalement disappears \'97 the vassals leave immediately, because they were never there voluntarily.\
