I think our first clue is in how high these empires were lifted up rhetorically. The greatness that tempts to reliance is real, and going beneath the ground from a maximal description of that greatness allows the sharpest possible rhetorical fall to jar loose reliance that listeners may have already fallen prey to. As support for this, we see the whom arc from its starting question given nearly exactly this interpretation in verse 18. To whom do you compare like thus, among the glory and greatness of the trees of Eden? But you will be brought down with the trees of Eden to the earth - the lowest in the midst of the uncircumcised.\
This, and all that precedes, is then wrapped up by saying it applies to or refers to the hubris of Pharaoh.\
Going backwards then, second, there is the mourning, in darkness and wilting - for we are still in the metaphor of trees. As with the oracles against Tyre, both the fear and the way those who depended on Egypt suffer when what they relied on is brought low, is portrayed to be instructive.\
Third then, is the continued theme of the LORD's sovereignty even over the graves of nations. The deep is back, whether as a removal of supply, or as flood.
