\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Going again to the text of chapter 17 not just for details of how Ezekiel uses pieces of tree imagery, but the whole message, I look to the end, and the shelter given. \
And I see, not an alternative to nations, for the sprig grows into a tree, but certainly a challenge to all existing nations. What the great empires, as they are now, claim to do, but fail because they fall to Sheol, the LORD will do. One nation which he plants in a particular way will truly provide shade, and as Daniel picks up Ezekiel 31, fruit. This will result in knowledge of the LORD. \
And the character of the LORD thus revealed is his bringing low the high tree, as we see in Ezekiel 31, but also bringing high the low tree. That alternative tree, that bringing high the low, is not at all foregrounded in Ezekiel 31. Ezekiel 31 foregrounds the bringing low the high tree. Drying up the green tree. That the LORD is the one who controls the water supply, as it were. But with Ezekiel 17 in the background - and the similarity gives us reason to believe it intended background - the bringing low the high tree is far from arbitrary, its purpose, context, and place in the character of the LORD already having been explained.\
On the other hand, the details of the water system are more implicit in chapter 17, but made more explicit in 31.\
As for what you say on the water system in verse 4, yes, the grammar points to the water, not the tree, as the cause of growth. And all the trees of Eden being parallel with those who drink water is hinted at, prepared for, with the earlier water channel metaphors.\
And yes, Eden with trees is explicitly mentioned, as are rivers watering the garden, so I think a reference to Genesis 2 is fully supported. At the very least, the water belonging to LORD as gardener is fully intended. Though I imagine if we went to the text again, whether Genesis 2 or Ezekiel 31, we would find that more is intended. That is to say, while I might do that myself, whichever of us does so, it is to the resources in the text we must turn.
