\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 That what happens to Egypt will bring revelation of the LORD's character is confirmed by Ezekiel 29, which says that rather directly. Ezekiel 31 then is not so much saying that this will happen, as this has already been said, as explaining how. Though we have yet to be clear about exactly how.\
That the beauty of Ezekiel 31 is intended with reference to that of Genesis 2 is less clear. Genesis 2 does have that beauty. But the words used to describe that beauty are, as you say, pleasing to the sight.\
And it is all the trees which are beautiful, not just the tree of life, or the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. The serpent talked up the tree of good and evil and its fruit. Eve talked up the tree and its fruit. But the text itself knows nothing special about that tree except that it is forbidden. That the tree and its fruit bring gain is a lie; the text says eating is disobedience and death.\
On the other hand, Daniel 4 does seem to bring in the tree of life when adapting Ezekiel 31, in that he brings in fruit. And Scripture from that point forward continues that idea. But that is after Ezekiel, and thus can only give us a permitted application, not the original meaning.\
Still, none of that precludes you finding additional evidence of a connection to Genesis 3.
