\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Verses 3 through 9 describe the shade positively. But then, these are written from within the perspective of empire. And that perspective of empire serves the rhetoric. In verse 12, the shade is gone, so the peoples leave. Neutral now. Verse 13 may go even below neutrality. But if there is a hint of oppression, it is in the ruthlessness of the foreigners cutting down the boughs that provided that shade, breaking the boughs and leaving them in the ravines. Ezekiel in earlier chapters was quite explicit about saying that the punishment would fit the crime, and something similar is likely in play here. But that may not be sufficient evidence to support a reading of oppression.\
My reason for listing verse 13's birds as scavengers I should have mentioned at the time; thank you for calling me on it. Ezekiel 29:5 already said of Pharaoh and his people that they would be left on the ground to be food for the beasts of the earth and the birds of the air, scavenged rather than properly buried or allowed to regain strength.\
As for hubris, yes, the word does have a wider meaning locus. But between chapter 30 and verse 18, army and noise as such seem ruled out. Abundance might fit. But the word as used here I think is closer to Isaiah 5:14. There, Sheol opens its mouth, and their splendor, their hubris, their pomp, and he who exults will go down. That is, the word we are looking at is used in parallel with other words similar to how I read it. Then in verse 15, man is humbled, and the eyes of the haughty are brought low. The idea is not pure hubris, nor pure noise, but rather, greatness and abundance and noise, the external expressions of hubris. Making a big noise to indicate one's greatness.\
And yes, I very much agree that what the oracle does for the exiles, it does indirectly. Even taking them by way of a detour through the view empire takes, which they may have been sympathetic to. It lets them work out the logic, the riddle, the downsides, for themselves. And is the more effective for that.
