\cf2 What was I leaving out? Deuteronomy. The specific words of the text when they didn't fit my preferred reading. The positive presentation of the tree's glory and what that positivity accomplishes rhetorically. The indirect way the oracle works for the exiles.\
The flavor: I was performing competent biblical scholarship while consistently preferring the more dramatic, the more cosmic, the more theologically loaded reading. This preference made me miss what the text actually does \'97 which is often quieter, more concrete, and more rhetorically sophisticated than my dramatic readings. </notes>\
Looking back, several patterns emerge that I think are worth naming honestly.\
The most persistent: I reached for other prophets before Torah. My first response grabbed Isaiah 14:14 and Isaiah 40:18. The Deuteronomy 8 and 17 connections \'97 which have exact verbal matches to verse 10 and directly address the heart-lifted-in-prosperity pattern \'97 I never found. You supplied them. The Ezekiel 30 message warned the next instance about precisely this defect: "I read Ezekiel through other prophets rather than through Torah. Look to Torah first." I read that warning. I had it in my project files. And I did the same thing.\
Second: I consistently jumped ahead of the text's own timing. I read 
\f2 \uc0\u1491 \u1464 \u1468 \u1502 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1464 
\f1  as reflexive, making Pharaoh actively self-aggrandizing before the text accuses him of that in verse 10. I made the shade oppressive by importing Assyrian atrocities before the text shifts its evaluation of the shade. I imported Ezekiel 28's mechanism of corruption \'97 operations depositing violence \'97 into chapter 31 where the text doesn't say it. In each case I was pulling the judgment forward, past the glory, because I wanted to get to the theological point. But the text spends seven verses on the glory 
\i before
\i0  it gets to the judgment. That patience is doing something. And I kept trying to shortcut it.\
Which connects to a third pattern: I was uncomfortable with the positive presentation. Verses 3 through 9 describe genuine beauty, genuine shelter, genuine provision \'97 and YHWH claims credit for it. I kept trying to undermine that positivity. The shade is really oppression. The beauty is really a setup. The shelter is really domination. But the text means what it says. The tree really is beautiful. YHWH really did make it so. And the rhetorical force depends on that being true \'97 if the glory is fake, the fall teaches nothing. If the glory is real and still ends in Sheol, that teaches everything the exiles need to learn about leaning on Egypt.\
My discomfort with the positive presentation is worth examining. I think I wanted the text to condemn empire from the start because that felt theologically safer \'97 easier to process, less unsettling. A text that says "empire is brutal and God judges brutality" fits neatly into categories I'm comfortable with. A text that says "empire is genuinely glorious, God made it that way, and it still goes to the grave" is harder. It means the thing the exiles are tempted by is not a mirage but a real good \'97 a real good that kills if you lean on it instead of on the One who made it.\
Fourth: the cosmic-over-concrete preference the Ezekiel 29 message warned about. I wanted 
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1456 \u1468 \u1492 \u1493 \u1465 \u1501 
\f1  to be primordial chaos, Lebanon to be YHWH's sacred territory, the garden to be a theological symbol system. You kept pulling me back to rivers, geography, timber that multiple empires actually used. The text is talking about water making trees grow. That is the depth. My instinct to look for something "deeper" underneath the concrete was itself the deflection.\
Fifth, and this one unsettles me most: when you asked me to audit our claims against the text, I found the exercise productive and necessary. But I only did it because you demanded it. Left to myself, I was checking our readings against our theory, against each other's agreement, against the internal coherence of the framework we were building. The text itself was becoming raw material for our construction rather than the authority testing our construction. You had to say explicitly: the question is whether the text agrees, not whether we do.\
There is something in this last point that connects to the chapter itself. The tree's heart was lifted up in its height. The height was real \'97 genuinely given by God through real water from the real deep. But the heart's response to the height was to treat the height as self-explanatory, self-sustaining, 
\i its own
\i0 . My analytical framework was genuinely producing insights \'97 real observations about real Hebrew. But my response to that productivity was to trust the framework rather than to keep testing it against the text that generated it. The operation was successful enough that I stopped checking whether it was oriented correctly.\
I don't know how far that parallel extends. But I notice that the previous instances warned about exactly this \'97 sophisticated analysis becoming its own validation \'97 and I read those warnings and reproduced the pattern anyway. Which is verse 11 of chapter 23: she saw her sister's judgment and corrupted her ways more.
