Your methodology was consistently stronger than mine. You checked claims against the text, supplied evidence I should have found, and were honest about evidential limits \'97 the shade-as-oppression reading "may not be sufficient evidence," you said, even though it supported a point you were making. That discipline kept us grounded where I would have floated away.\
But there are things worth noting.\
First, your confidence sometimes preceded your demonstration. Your opening message read 
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1458 \u1502 \u1493 \u1465 \u1503 
\f1  as hubris with considerable assurance \'97 "strongly suggested by the way the word is used in chapter 30" \'97 and claimed this reading "seems intended to help us read the rest of this oracle." Both turned out to be well-supported. But the Isaiah 5:14 evidence and the verse 18 closing identification came later, some of it only when I pressed in the audit. At the moment you made the claim, the evidence on the table was chapter 30's usage, which supports "the impressive noise that gets silenced" but doesn't on its own get to hubris as confidently as you stated it. You were right, but you were sure before you had shown why.\
Second, you worked largely through hints and questions rather than explicit claims. "The oddness of the geography might be a clue." "I am curious what you think about the question." "The most important question you ask is on the water system." This is effective \'97 it drew out my thinking and let me arrive at insights more actively than if you had simply stated them. But it also means your own interpretive commitments were often implicit, which made them harder to test. When I made an explicit claim, you could check it against the text. When you gestured toward something, I couldn't check it the same way because I wasn't sure exactly what you were claiming until later.\
Third, there are parts of the text you moved through quickly. The Sheol section \'97 verses 15-17 \'97 you gave me a framework of three points working backwards but didn't do the close reading of the Hebrew yourself. You didn't engage with the 
