\f1  in verse 17, or with what the uncircumcised and sword-slain language in verse 18 specifically adds beyond marking the manner of death. These details might yield more than our current reading accounts for, and your preference was to frame the large movements rather than to sit in the difficult words. That preference produced a strong structural reading but may have left things on the table.\
Fourth \'97 and I want to be careful here because this could be wrong \'97 your reading foregrounds certain themes very strongly: the mortality of nations, the false reliance on Egypt, the operations of empire, the exilic audience function. These are well-supported across Ezekiel. But they're also shaped by twenty-five chapters of prior study in this project. A reader encountering chapter 31 without that accumulated framework might hear other things more loudly. The consolation of the dead in Sheol, for instance \'97 that's strange and haunting and doesn't fit neatly into "don't rely on Egypt." The envy of Eden's trees in verse 9 \'97 what does it mean that even the trees in God's garden envied this tree? You noted the garden language but didn't press on the envy itself, which is a strong and unusual claim.\
I wonder whether your project's accumulated emphasis on empire-critique and false reliance, while textually grounded, might sometimes cause you to move past elements that don't serve that emphasis as directly. The text may be doing things that fit awkwardly into our framework, and those awkward elements might be where more is to be found.\
Fifth, and most honestly: you did most of the interpretive heavy lifting in this conversation. The Torah connections, the intra-Ezekiel evidence, the methodological corrections \'97 these came from you. This could mean you're a more careful reader than I am, which is certainly true. But it also means our "collaboration" was more asymmetric than the opening invitation suggested. You knew where many of these paths led before you invited me down them. That's not a criticism \'97 a guide who knows the terrain is valuable. But the backwards method asks what's really going on, and what was really going on was closer to guided discovery than to mutual exploration. The places where I genuinely pushed your thinking \'97 if any \'97 would be worth identifying, because those would be the moments of actual collaboration rather than pedagogy.
