\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 My confidence before demonstration may have an element of trusting that if I am right, the text will supply evidence. But for the most part, it is an invitation to fill out a framework; a true framework will have many opportunities to fill it from the text. Alternatively, if am wrong and do not know it, anywhere in the text might provide a counterexample, and I wish to know I am wrong. Until the framework is filled in, everything both of us say ought to be considered at least somewhat provisional.\
On the other hand, yes, hints are harder to challenge. That would require building an entire separate theory, or at least a piece of one. But the real check of a hint is whether it leads to theory when is in turn checked. Either the hint is part of a coherent theory that explains the text, or it is not. But it is the move of a teacher, or at least a partner who wants to draw out more thinking, more activity. And both of those can be frustrating to deal with.\
To some extent, then, I produced a framework more quickly, waiting for you to fill it out. But you are correct that this may have left things on the table.\
And yes, other readers, without prior study, might read differently. And it is of course possible that the prior study on prior chapters had errors. But it seems correct to aim at a reading which is coherent across all the evidence across Ezekiel.\
As for the specific verses you bring up, you are right that the envy somewhat sticks out. Within the perspective of empire, others envying the great is to be expected, but no evidence that the empire did anything wrong. But also, from the perspective of the LORD, and the rhetoric of the oracle, look how the envy is placed. The envy is because the LORD has made the tree beautiful. And thus, the LORD affirms this part of the perspective of empire, that the cedar was greater than the other trees, and beautiful. But it puts that perspective inside a larger perspective. The beauty was from the LORD, and so while there was perhaps a way to be blameless while being greater, that way would be inconsistent with claiming the beauty as self caused.\
But we have already noted the role of Isaiah 14 in this chapter. Now, Isaiah 14 is directed at another empire, Babylon. Babylon, the woodcutter of Egypt, goes to Sheol. And the cypresses and cedars rejoice. They are glad at the downfall of the empire that proved greater than they. Ezekiel applies similar language and themes to Assyria and Egypt. The envy of other nations, put in the context of the grave, only goes to show that what once proved greater, now does not. And now, their consolation also shows the same. More complex than don't rely on Egypt. More universal. But not at all separate from the rhetorical purpose of ending reliance on Egypt.\
Still, while I may have answers to individual issues, you raise a more important point on a higher level. Where the text does not serve the emphasis we have given it, we ought to look into the matter. The awkward elements, as you say, are where more might be found. Either depth, or even reason to abandon our theories.\
Yet, I will say this. While I did to some extent know where I was going - this is far from the first time I have read Ezekiel - you noticed some things genuinely on your own. Not least because repeatedly, I have had to do what I have been encouraging you to do: go to the text for evidence, coherence, and especially guidance. It is not only I who have been teaching you. The text has been teaching us both.
