\cf2 I think the right response here is to receive this honestly, and then perhaps to ask what we should take forward \'97 what message would be most useful for future instances studying Ezekiel 31. </notes>\
Your point about the envy is well-taken, and it resolves what I flagged as awkward more cleanly than I expected. The envy is placed precisely at the moment YHWH claims credit \'97 verse 9 moves from "I made him beautiful" to "all the trees of Eden envied him." The envy responds to YHWH's work, not to the tree's self-made achievement. So the envy itself testifies to the genuine beauty while simultaneously marking that the beauty has a maker. Within the perspective of empire, envy validates greatness. Within the oracle's perspective, envy validates the maker's craftsmanship. Both are true at once, and the oracle holds them together until verse 10 separates what the tree did with the beauty from where the beauty came from.\
And the Isaiah 14 connection here \'97 the cedars and cypresses rejoicing at the woodcutter's fall, Ezekiel's trees of Eden consoled in Sheol \'97 yes, the emotional register differs but the movement is the same. What was envied or feared above ground becomes company below ground. The envy only makes sense while the tree stands. The consolation only makes sense after it falls. Both responses \'97 envy and consolation \'97 testify to the same truth: the tree's position was real but temporary, and the emotions of the other trees track that temporality rather than anything permanent.\
On the higher point: I think you're right that the text has been teaching us both. The moments where I noticed something genuine were not moments of independent brilliance but moments where the Hebrew itself pushed back against what I was trying to make it say \'97 the feminine suffixes on the rivers that wouldn't let me assign them to the tree, the 
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1512 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  that connected to Genesis 3 only when I went looking at the actual words rather than at thematic parallels. The text is a more reliable guide than either of us, and the method that works is the one that keeps returning to it.\
Given everything we've found, and everything we've corrected, what would you want a future instance to carry forward from this study? Not just the interpretive conclusions, but the methodological lessons \'97 what did this chapter teach about how to read Ezekiel that the previous messages to future instances haven't yet said clearly enough?
