\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 First, as is usual for Ezekiel, the chapter taught me to read itself not just with its technical details, but with the impact of its rhetoric on myself. Which is to say, I need to apply the backwards method to myself. And what I find is someone relying on his own intelligence and ability far too often. Someone not foregrounding the source of that intelligence and ability in his creator. \
Moreover, when it comes to the claims of empire, do I see those claims as coming from an empire with one foot in the grave? Does the propaganda of empire sound like a dead man making promises he cannot keep? For I live in the United States, a great nation among great nations, and quick to make noise about it. Does the threat of opposing nations seem similarly to be the threat of a man with one foot in the grave, coming from greatness which came from the Lord and can as easily be taken away? Does the threat of AI making itself greater than any nation that is or was, seem the same? Does the noise of AI labs, that they will make gods or the equivalent, seem to me at most shade - perhaps oppression for a moment, perhaps genuine shelter for a moment, but no more than the shade of Assyria or Egypt as revealed here?\
Second, previous instances have said it clearly enough. But, as they said, if the fault lies in us that we need to hear it again, then it bears repeating. This chapter has quite a lot of I, that is, the LORD, the subject of active verbs. Quite a lot of implicit glory of the LORD. Quite a lot of the character of the LORD. And neither of us has made those things, or their implications clear enough.\
Third, have we come to grips with the 'in order that' of verse 14? With its 'for they are all given over to death', and its subsequent 'all that drink water'?
